Fever of Unknown Origin (FUO)
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Temperature >37.5°C (101°F) multiple times | Duration >3 weeks | No definitive diagnosis after 1 week of targeted workup

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-005
Reason for Visit: Persistent high fevers accompanied by chest tightness requiring urgent exclusion of acute cardiac pathology or severe systemic infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate three weeks of persistent fevers culminating in new-onset chest tightness. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out acute coronary syndrome or infective endocarditis to prevent catastrophic deterioration prior to the next available in-hours consultation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I keep having these terrible fevers every night, and now my chest feels tight and heavy even when I am just resting.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports recurrent and persistent temperature elevation exceeding 38.3 C over an extended period. The duration of the fever has lasted at least three weeks. The patient describes nonspecific malaise and generalized weakness. There are profound night sweats requiring a change of clothes multiple times a week. The patient notes an unintentional weight loss of 3 kilograms, migratory arthralgia, and myalgia. In the past 12 hours, the patient developed intermittent chills, rigors, and non-exertional chest tightness. The patient confirms normal baseline diagnostic evaluations were completed prior to this acute flare.
Functional Status: Independent, but severely limited by profound fatigue.
Social/Safety Concerns: Anxiety regarding lack of definitive diagnosis.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 118/72 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 38 C | RR: 18 /min | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: The patient appears flushed, chronically fatigued, and mildly distressed by the chest tightness.
Physical Exam: Comprehensive physical examination demonstrates a stark lack of localizing signs. The chest is clear to auscultation bilaterally. Cardiovascular examination reveals a regular rhythm with no murmurs, rubs, or gallops. Abdomen is soft without hepatosplenomegaly. Skin lacks rashes or petechiae. The patient displays non-exertional chest tightness. Point-of-care testing reveals a normal ECG and negative cardiac biomarkers (Troponin).
Environmental Observations: Clean, safe environment. Fan running continuously due to the patient's hyperthermia.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking paracetamol with minimal relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Fever of Unknown Origin (FUO) - Status: Exacerbated by acute chest tightness and systemic inflammatory response.
Differentials:

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out strictly due to the completely normal ECG, negative cardiac biomarkers, and atypical nature of the chest tightness.

Infective Endocarditis - Ruled out clinically for tonight's acute risk due to the absence of new murmurs or peripheral stigmata, though requires formal echocardiography for definitive exclusion.

Acute Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out due to normal oxygen saturation, clear lung fields, and normal respiratory rate in the absence of pleuritic pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours assessment due to the high-risk combination of prolonged, unremitting fevers and new-onset chest tightness. Telephone triage appropriately escalated the presentation given the acute risk of myocardial infarction, pericarditis, or evolving septic shock. Rigorous clinical examination combined with point-of-care ECG and biomarker testing definitively ruled out an acute ischemic cardiac event. Securing this objective data prevented an unnecessary emergency department admission for a chronic, undifferentiated systemic issue while ensuring absolute patient safety overnight until comprehensive outpatient diagnostics can resume.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral NSAIDs for symptomatic fever control. The expected investigation results include negative blood cultures, negative autoantibodies, and non-diagnostic baseline imaging, which will assist in defining the parameters of the diagnosis.
Positive Behavioral Change: To monitor temperature trends accurately, record your temperature every evening before dinner and log it in a dedicated journal; visualizing the stabilized trend will provide positive reinforcement and reduce anxiety regarding unexplained fevers.
Home-Based Services: None indicated tonight.
DME/Safety: Provision of a digital thermometer for accurate home tracking.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48 hours for a coordinated referral to an infectious disease or rheumatology specialist.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the chest pain becomes crushing, radiates to the jaw or arm, is accompanied by shortness of breath, or if the patient becomes confused or unrousable.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending exhaustive serological and radiological investigations; systemic conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course involves continued cyclic fevers until the underlying etiology is identified, favoring meticulous outpatient investigation over disjointed emergency admissions.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency attention if you develop a new, severe headache, neck stiffness, an unblanching skin rash, or extreme difficulty catching your breath.

